WEBVTT

00:04.650 --> 00:07.225
Every time rock is drilled, blasted,

00:07.225 --> 00:11.025
crushed, screened or driven over, it makes dust.

00:11.025 --> 00:13.225
The dust you can see settles out on your truck

00:13.225 --> 00:16.163
and your boots. The dust that matters most is the

00:16.163 --> 00:19.350
part you can't see: particles so fine they float

00:19.350 --> 00:22.012
for a long time and get breathed deep into your

00:22.012 --> 00:24.825
lungs. That's called respirable dust.

00:25.969 --> 00:28.456
Some rock contains crystalline silica,

00:28.456 --> 00:31.831
mostly as quartz. When respirable silica dust gets

00:31.831 --> 00:34.356
into the lungs, the body can't clear it,

00:34.356 --> 00:37.968
and over years it causes scarring called silicosis.

00:37.968 --> 00:40.944
Silicosis makes it harder and harder to breathe.

00:40.944 --> 00:43.694
It has no cure, and it keeps getting worse even

00:43.694 --> 00:47.144
after the exposure stops. Silica exposure is also

00:47.144 --> 00:50.231
linked to lung cancer, other lung disease and kidney

00:50.231 --> 00:53.294
disease. The damage takes years to show,

00:53.294 --> 00:55.294
which is why you protect yourself now,

00:55.294 --> 00:56.544
when you feel fine.

00:57.654 --> 01:00.954
Limestone generally contains less quartz than sandstone

01:00.954 --> 01:03.442
or granite, but how much is in the rock at a given

01:03.442 --> 01:06.066
place isn't something you can tell by looking.

01:06.066 --> 01:08.379
That's why dust is controlled everywhere,

01:08.379 --> 01:11.554
and why MSHA samples the air miners breathe.

01:14.872 --> 01:17.222
Drilling is one of the biggest sources.

01:17.222 --> 01:20.122
Drills use a dust collector or water to keep dust

01:20.122 --> 01:22.847
down at the hole. If the collector isn't working

01:22.847 --> 01:25.734
or the water runs out, the dust goes into the air

01:25.734 --> 01:28.847
around the drill and the operator. Report it and

01:28.847 --> 01:31.534
don't keep drilling dry. After a blast,

01:31.534 --> 01:34.209
the air in the heading or the pit is full of dust

01:34.209 --> 01:37.234
and fumes. Stay out until it's cleared and you're

01:37.234 --> 01:38.434
allowed back in.

01:39.562 --> 01:42.262
Crushers, screens and conveyor transfer points

01:42.262 --> 01:45.687
throw dust every time rock drops or breaks.

01:45.687 --> 01:48.224
Our Liwell plants at Boonesboro and Clover Bottom

01:48.224 --> 01:51.174
are dry screen plants, like a wash plant without

01:51.174 --> 01:54.299
the water, so they need their own dust controls.

01:54.299 --> 01:57.812
Spray bars, covers, skirts and enclosures keep

01:57.812 --> 02:01.099
dust at the source. Report a plugged spray nozzle,

02:01.099 --> 02:04.049
a torn skirt or a missing cover just like any other

02:04.049 --> 02:04.987
defect.

02:06.112 --> 02:08.737
Haul roads make dust whenever they dry out,

02:08.737 --> 02:11.512
on the surface and underground. Watering keeps

02:11.512 --> 02:14.275
it down. Cleanup can be one of the dustiest jobs

02:14.275 --> 02:17.287
on site if it's done wrong. Don't dry sweep and

02:17.287 --> 02:20.187
don't blow dust off equipment, or yourself,

02:20.187 --> 02:22.500
with compressed air. That just puts it back in

02:22.500 --> 02:25.037
the air at face level. Wet it down,

02:25.037 --> 02:27.487
vacuum it, or shovel it carefully.

02:28.606 --> 02:31.131
Underground, the same sources are packed into a

02:31.131 --> 02:34.831
smaller space. Face drilling, mucking and loading,

02:34.831 --> 02:37.768
and traffic on dry headings all make dust,

02:37.768 --> 02:40.069
and the ventilation air carries it down the heading

02:40.069 --> 02:42.831
to wherever people are working. When you can,

02:42.831 --> 02:45.569
work upwind of the dust source, keep water on the

02:45.569 --> 02:48.518
drill and the muck pile, and don't park or take

02:48.518 --> 02:50.969
a break downwind of a loading face.

02:50.969 --> 02:52.994
If you notice dust hanging in a heading that's

02:52.994 --> 02:56.119
usually clear, the ventilation may have changed.

02:56.119 --> 02:56.981
Report it.

03:00.321 --> 03:02.284
Here's where the rule stands today.

03:02.284 --> 03:06.796
MSHA enforces the exposure limits in 30 CFR 56

03:06.796 --> 03:11.946
and 57.5001 for metal and nonmetal mines like ours.

03:11.946 --> 03:15.883
In 2024, MSHA published a new silica rule that

03:15.883 --> 03:19.008
would lower the limit. In April 2025,

03:19.008 --> 03:21.371
a federal appeals court stayed compliance with

03:21.371 --> 03:25.221
that rule, and in April 2026, MSHA delayed the

03:25.221 --> 03:28.184
metal and nonmetal changes indefinitely while the

03:28.184 --> 03:31.133
court case continues. So the existing standards

03:31.133 --> 03:33.909
are the ones in force. If that changes,

03:33.909 --> 03:36.409
your training will be updated. Either way,

03:36.409 --> 03:38.659
the way you protect yourself is the same.

03:39.770 --> 03:45.132
Under 56 and 57.5005, dust is controlled first

03:45.132 --> 03:48.532
by preventing it, removing it with exhaust ventilation,

03:48.532 --> 03:51.145
or diluting it with clean air. That means water

03:51.145 --> 03:54.857
sprays, dust collectors, covers and ventilation.

03:54.857 --> 03:57.545
For you, it also means the cab. If your machine

03:57.545 --> 04:00.370
has an enclosed cab with filters, it protects you

04:00.370 --> 04:03.007
only if the doors and windows stay shut.

04:03.007 --> 04:05.807
Running with the window down on a dusty road throws

04:05.807 --> 04:08.845
that protection away. Report a cab filter or seal

04:08.845 --> 04:09.995
that isn't working.

04:11.120 --> 04:13.745
Respirators are used when engineering controls

04:13.745 --> 04:16.233
haven't been developed yet, while controls are

04:16.233 --> 04:18.858
being put in place, or when the nature of the job

04:18.858 --> 04:21.758
requires it, like occasional maintenance in a dusty

04:21.758 --> 04:24.946
area. Then the respirator has to be NIOSH-approved

04:24.946 --> 04:27.396
and suitable for the job, and it's used under a

04:27.396 --> 04:30.171
program that covers selection, training,

04:30.171 --> 04:33.358
fitting, cleaning and maintenance. At every Allen

04:33.358 --> 04:36.433
site, respirators are required outside the screen

04:36.433 --> 04:39.108
plant while it's running. At Boonesboro,

04:39.108 --> 04:42.108
certain shop tasks and cleaning tasks also require

04:42.108 --> 04:45.283
one. Your instructor will show you exactly where,

04:45.283 --> 04:47.508
and how the respirator program works.

04:47.508 --> 04:50.483
A respirator only works if it seals to your face,

04:50.483 --> 04:53.083
so follow your fit and seal-check training every

04:53.083 --> 04:53.833
time.

04:54.950 --> 04:58.400
MSHA inspectors take dust samples by having a miner

04:58.400 --> 05:01.213
wear a small pump on the belt, with the inlet clipped

05:01.213 --> 05:03.675
near the breathing zone, for the shift.

05:03.675 --> 05:05.700
If you're asked to wear one, wear it the whole

05:05.700 --> 05:08.875
shift, do your normal work, and don't cover it,

05:08.875 --> 05:11.488
remove it or move it away from your face.

05:11.488 --> 05:14.000
A sample is only useful if it shows what you really

05:14.000 --> 05:16.363
breathe. A sample that comes out high isn't your

05:16.363 --> 05:19.188
fault. It tells everyone where controls need to

05:19.188 --> 05:20.125
improve.

05:21.248 --> 05:23.286
Dust you carry home in your clothes,

05:23.286 --> 05:25.848
your truck and your hair keeps exposing you,

05:25.848 --> 05:28.573
and your family, after the shift. Clean off the

05:28.573 --> 05:31.436
right way before you leave, with a vacuum or by

05:31.436 --> 05:33.686
changing clothes if that's available,

05:33.686 --> 05:36.536
not with compressed air. Keep your lunch and drinks

05:36.536 --> 05:39.298
away from dusty surfaces. And tell your doctor

05:39.298 --> 05:41.773
that you work around rock dust. If you develop

05:41.773 --> 05:44.636
a cough that won't go away, shortness of breath

05:44.636 --> 05:47.711
or wheezing, get checked and mention your work.

05:48.855 --> 05:51.367
So here's your part. Keep cab doors and windows

05:51.367 --> 05:54.430
shut. Report broken sprays, collectors,

05:54.430 --> 05:57.755
covers and cab filters. Don't dry sweep or blow

05:57.755 --> 06:00.730
off with air. Stay out of a heading or pit after

06:00.730 --> 06:03.630
a blast until it clears. And wear your respirator

06:03.630 --> 06:06.267
when you're directed to. Silicosis takes years

06:06.267 --> 06:09.092
to show up. The habits you build now decide whether

06:09.092 --> 06:10.192
it ever does.

06:11.324 --> 06:14.487
Remember: your site's plans and procedures always

06:14.487 --> 06:15.987
override this video.
